#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079625 :tutorial/content "Consider the following sequence:\n\n$$\n1,\\quad\\:\\: 3,\\quad\\:\\: 9,\\quad\\:\\: 27,\\quad\\:\\: 81,\\quad\\:\\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nNotice that each term is ${\\color{blue}3}$ times the previous term:\n\n![](images/9a/9a3af8dd763aa0ab6b176a3a9d711ead4d029e2cebef823f906403be5fc38786.png)\n\nA sequence like the one above, where each term is found by multiplying the previous term by a fixed constant, is called a **geometric sequence**. \n\nThis fixed constant is called the **common ratio**, and is denoted by $r.$ In this particular case, the common ratio is $r={\\color{blue}3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "406573ab-cd46-52f5-9dca-f559f83bb0af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1667 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
